'''Old Tibetan''' refers to the earliest attested form of [[Tibetic languages|Tibetan language]], reflected in documents from the adoption of writing by the [[Tibetan Empire]] in the mid-7th century to the early 9th century. In {{CE|816}}, during the reign of Tibetan King [[Sadnalegs]], literary Tibetan underwent comprehensive standardization, resulting in [[Classical Tibetan]].{{sfn|Hodge|1993|p=vii}}

==Phonology==
Old Tibetan is characterised by many features that are lost in Classical Tibetan, including ''my-'' rather than ''m-'' before the vowels ''-i-'' and ''-e-'', the cluster ''sts-'' which simplifies to ''s-'' in Classical Tibetan, and a reverse form of the vowel letter for ''i'' (''gi-gu'').{{sfn|Hill|2010a|pp=117–118}} Aspiration was not phonemic and many words were written indiscriminately with consonants from the aspirated or unaspirated series. Most consonants could be palatalized, and the palatal series from the Tibetan script represents palatalized coronals. The sound conventionally transcribed with the letter འ ([[Wylie transliteration|Wylie]]: 'a) was a voiced velar fricative, while the voiceless rhotic and lateral are written with digraphs ཧྲ {{angbr|hr}} and ལྷ {{angbr|lh}}. Unlike virtually all modern Tibetan languages, the Old Tibetan orthography did not contain [[silent letter]]s, and the words were pronounced as written.

