=== Syllable structure ===
In Old Tibetan, syllables can be quite complex with up to three consonants in the onset, two glides, and two coda consonants. This structure can be represented as {{tlit|bo|italic=no|'''(C<sub>1</sub>C<sub>2</sub>)C<sub>3</sub>(G<sub>1</sub>G<sub>2</sub>)V(C<sub>4</sub>C<sub>5</sub>)'''}}, with all positions except '''C<sub>3</sub>''' and '''V''' optional. This allows for complicated syllables like བསྒྲིགས {{tlit|bo|bsgrigs}} "arranged" and འདྲྭ ''<nowiki/>'drwa'' "web", for which the pronunciations {{IPA|[βzɡriks]}} and {{IPA|[ɣdrʷa]}} can be reconstructed.

A voicing contrast only exists in slot '''C<sub>3</sub>''' and spreads to '''C<sub>1</sub>''' and '''C<sub>2</sub>''' so སྒོ ''sgo'' "door" would be realized as {{IPA|[zɡo]}} while སྐུ {{tlit|bo|sku}} "body" would be {{IPA|[sku]}}. Final consonants are always voiceless e.g. འཛིནད་ ''<nowiki/>'dzind'' {{IPA|[ɣd͡zint]}} and གཟུགས་ {{tlit|bo|gzugs}} [{{tlit|bo|gzuks}}]. The phoneme {{IPAslink|b}} in '''C<sub>1</sub>''' was likely realized as {{IPAblink|ɸ}} (or {{IPAblink|β}} when '''C<sub>3</sub>''' is voiced) e.g. བསྒྲེ {{tlit|bo|bsgre}} {{IPA|[βzɡre]}} and བརྩིས {{tlit|bo|brtsis}} {{IPA|[ɸrtˢis]}}. The features of [[Palatalization (phonetics)|palatalization]] {{IPAslink|i̯}} {{IPA|[Cʲ]}} and [[labialization]] {{IPAslink|w}} {{IPA|[Cʷ]}} can be considered separate phonemes, realized as glides in '''G<sub>1</sub>''' and '''G<sub>2</sub>''' respectively. Only certain consonants are permitted in some syllable slots, as summarized below:{{sfn|Hill|2010a|pp=121–122}}

