=== Palatalization ===
Palatalization {{IPA|/Cʲ/}} was phonemically distinct from the onset cluster {{IPA|/Cj/}}. This produces a contrast between གཡ {{angbr|g.y}} {{IPA|/ɡj/}} and གྱ {{angbr|gy}} {{IPA|/ɡʲ/}}, demonstrated by the minimal pair གཡང་ ''g.yaṅ'' "sheep" and གྱང་ ''gyaṅ'' "also, and".{{sfn|Hill|2012b}} The sounds written with the palatal letters ཅ c, ཇ j, ཉ ny, ཞ zh, and ཤ sh were palatalized counterparts of the phonemic sounds ཙ ts, ཛ dz, ན n, ཟ z, and ས s.{{sfn|Hill|2010a|p=118}}

==Morphology==

=== Nominal ===
[[grammatical case|Case]] markers are affixed to entire noun phrases, not to individual words (i.e. ''Gruppenflexion''). Old Tibetan distinguishes the same ten cases as [[Classical Tibetan]]:{{sfn|Hill|2012a}}

* [[absolutive case|absolutive]] (morphologically unmarked)
* [[genitive case|genitive]] (གི་ -''gi'',  གྱི་ -''kyi'', ཀྱི་ -{{tlit|bo|gyi}}, འི་ ''-'i'', ཡི་ -''yi'')
* [[agentive case|agentive]] (གིས་ -''gis'', གྱིས་ -''kyis'', ཀྱིས་ -{{tlit|bo|gyis}}, ས་ -''sa'', ཡིས་ -''yis'')
* [[locative case|locative]] (ན་ -''na'')
* [[allative case|allative]] (ལ་ -''la'')
* [[terminative case|terminative]] (རུ་ -''ru'', སུ་ -''su'', ཏུ་ -''tu'', དུ་ -''du'', ར་ -''ra'')
* [[comitative case|comitative]] (དང་ -''dang'')
* [[ablative case|ablative]] (ནས་ -''nas'')
* [[elative case|elative]] (ལས་ -''las'')
* [[comparative case|comparative]] (བས་ -''bas'')

However, whereas the locative, allative, and terminative gradually fell together in Classical Tibetan (and are referred to the indigenous grammatical tradition as the ''la don bdun''), in Old Tibetan these three cases are clearly distinguished.{{sfn|Hill|2011}} Traditional Tibetan grammarians do not distinguish case markers in this manner, but rather distribute these case morphemes (excluding ''-dang'' and ''-bas'') into the eight cases of [[Sanskrit language|Sanskrit]].

=== Verbal ===
Old Tibetan transitive verbs were inflected for up to four stems, while intransitive verbs only had one or two stems. In the [[active voice]], there was an [[imperfective]] stem and a [[perfective]] stem, corresponding to the [[Classical Tibetan]] present and past stems respectively. Transitive verbs also may have two [[passive voice]] stems, a dynamic stem and stative stem. These two stems in turn correspond to the Classical future and imperative stems.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Bialek|first1=Joanna|date=2020|title=Old Tibetan verb morphology and semantics: An attempt at a reconstruction|journal=Himalayan Linguistics|volume=19|issue=1|doi=10.5070/H919145017|s2cid=225424677 |url=https://escholarship.org/uc/item/5m40995x|doi-access=free}}</ref>

==Personal pronouns==
Old Tibetan has three first person singular pronouns {{lang|otb|ང}}་ ''ṅa'', {{lang|otb|བདག}}་ {{tlit|bo|bdag}}, and {{lang|otb|ཁོ་བོ}}་ {{tlit|bo|kho-bo}}, and three first-person plural pronouns {{lang|otb|ངེད}}་ {{tlit|bo|nged}}, {{lang|otb|བདག་ཅག}}་ {{tlit|bo|bdag-cag}}, and {{lang|otb|འོ་སྐོལ་}} {{tlit|bo|'o-skol}}. The second person pronouns include two singulars {{lang|otb|ཁྱོད་}} {{tlit|bo|khyod}} and {{lang|otb|ཁྱོ(ན)་འདའ་}}  {{tlit|bo|khyo(n)}}-'da' and a plural {{lang|otb|ཁྱེད་}} {{tlit|bo|khyed}}.{{sfn|Hill|2010b}}

